    fix(QA-DUCKBRAIN-005): repair the three suites the clean-machine battery reds
    
    Three independent failures at bbee2cc, each reproduced and root-caused
    before the fix (evidence: bunker battery agent 3bd89267, native.log held at
    /tmp/qa-duck-harvest/native.log, and the act CI log ci.log in the same
    harvest).
    
    1) src/schema/table-registry.test.ts t4 (REG-GONE-002) — the test induced a
       failed scan with `chmod 0o000` on the tables dir, but uid 0 bypasses
       DIRECTORY mode bits: under root (how the act/CI leg runs the suite) the
       chmod is a silent no-op, the scan SUCCEEDS, and the assertion sees
       `[{name:'late',...}]` instead of `[]` — the exact battery error
       ("expected [...] to deeply equal []", ci.log line 201). Reproduced by
       running the file as root. Fix: induce the failure with readdir's ENOTDIR
       under root (replace the tables dir with a regular file) and keep chmod
       0o000 for non-root, so the same "listing failed" condition holds for
       every uid; the declaration bytes are restored for the healing arm.
    
    2) src/duckdb/queries.test.ts hook timeout — the battery's 10 s hook failure
       is `afterEach` (line 28, closeDuckDB), reported against the file's first
       test, which took 10037 ms while the remaining ten tests took ~200-330 ms
       each: a one-time native cost. Root cause: loadVSSExtension uses
       `db.exec()` with no callback, which node-duckdb dispatches
       fire-and-forget, so initDuckDB resolves before INSTALL/LOAD vss has run
       and the first `db.close()` blocks until that in-flight work drains
       (measured: first close 1253 ms with a cold ~/.duckdb cache, 45 ms warm).
       vitest.config.ts already grants tests 15 s for exactly this native
       reality but left hooks at vitest's 10 s default. Fix: hookTimeout 30 s.
       Verified live: a 12 s hook passes with the repo config and fails with
       "--hookTimeout=10000".
    
    3) src/cli/unix-socket-flag.test.ts — no flag leak exists (the local guard is
       fine: the daemon prints "HTTP server listening on Unix socket <path>").
       The battery's exit-1 came from startup bookkeeping: without
       DUCKBRAIN_DATA_DIR the pidfile is the SHARED
       `<tmpdir>/duckbrain-http-<socket-basename>.pid`; the test SIGKILLs its
       child, so the file is never removed, and once one is owned by a different
       user (root-run instance/container/earlier battery) the daemon dies with
       "Failed to start HTTP server: EACCES ... pidfile" -> exit 1 before it
       binds anything. Reproduced exactly by leaving a root-owned
       /tmp/duckbrain-http-test.sock.pid in place.
       - test: give every spawned daemon its own DUCKBRAIN_DATA_DIR +
         DUCKBRAIN_NAMESPACES_PATH (the sibling CLI suites' hermeticity pattern),
         and replace `expect(code).not.toBe(1)` — true for ANY unrelated startup
         failure and silent about the flag — with the positive evidence that the
         HTTP path consumed the flag ("HTTP server started" + "listening on Unix
         socket <path>"). A new arm pins the product side: with
         DUCKBRAIN_DATA_DIR pointed at a regular file the pidfile write fails
         ENOTDIR for every uid (a chmod refusal is bypassed by root) and the
         daemon must degrade to a warning, not exit 1.
       - product (src/cli/http.ts): the pidfile write is now best-effort like
         cleanupStalePidFile — the listener is already bound, and a foreign-owned
         or unwritable pidfile must never take down a ready server. RED-proof:
         with the pre-fix http.ts the new arm fails ("Failed to start HTTP
         server: ENOTDIR ...", no socket banner); the other three arms pass on
         both trees, so they are not coupled to the product change.
    
    Verification: three suites 19/19 green (also green as root for the
    table-registry file, which is where t4 red); tsc --noEmit rc=0; full suite
    green; no writes to the repo's duckbrain.config.json (sha256 identical
    before/after) or namespaces/ (0 non-.git files newer than the run marker;
    the scheduler auto-commit that appears is the live daemon's 5-minute
    cadence).
    
    Residual (not fixed here, product-side, wider than this row): the pidfile
    name is derived from the socket BASENAME in a shared directory, so two
    instances whose sockets share a basename collide on one pidfile, and a
    foreign-owned leftover now only degrades bookkeeping instead of killing the
    daemon. Also loadVSSExtension remains fire-and-forget, so initDuckDB
    resolves before the extension is installed; awaiting it would move the cold
    cost into init (and change offline behavior), which is out of this row's
    blast radius.
    
